{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Spike7aParent",
    "description": "PLAN.md step 7a, the parent half, in the ONE spelling that evaluates under BOTH bindings. This document holds no model of its own: only document-level configuration — two `data_sources` and the two metaparameters their `extent` binds — and a single top-level `models` entry that is a `{ref}`. `age_group_census.esm` supplies every variable, every equation, every data BINDING, and its own shape; nothing here names a column or an arithmetic operation.\n\nWHAT 7a ESTABLISHES. A variable declared inside a ref'd child CAN carry `update: {kind: \"data\", source: \"X\"}` where `data_sources.X` is declared only in the mounting document. That is §7.1's load-bearing assumption and it holds: the two Parquet files are read, the equi-join runs, and the numbers are the default database's own (41 categories in 7 groups, ageIDs summing to 820, group 2099 holding 21). The child fails alone — it reads two sources it does not declare — and evaluates through this parent, which is §7.2's probe carried the one step further that §7.5 said was missing.\n\nTHE SHAPE MOVED ON 2026-09-12 AND THE NUMBERS DID NOT. As first written this document also declared the two `index_sets` that size the child's axes, and the child declared them open — sized by names only this file provided. EarthSciAST PR #298 resolves a mounted leaf in its own scope and refuses that; the Python binding had been refusing it all along, as `subsystem_index_set_conflict`, while Rust accepted it (finding F51). The axes and the metaparameters that size them now live in the child. This file keeps a copy of the metaparameters ONLY because of F50, below. The emitted relation is unchanged.\n\nTHREE THINGS §7.2 DID NOT SAY, each measured here and filed. The first has since been overtaken, and is kept because the workaround it forced is traceable through it.\n\n1. THE PARENT USED TO HAVE TO RESTATE THE CHILD'S `index_sets`. Without them `esm validate` PASSED and the build failed with `aggregate range 'g' references index set 'spike_agegroup_rows', which is not declared in the document index_sets registry`, because the interpreter's registry is `file.index_sets` — this document's own — and a mounted child's did not enter it. That was finding F46, and PR #298 closed it: there is no `index_sets` block in this file any more.\n\n2. THIS DOCUMENT MAY NOT HOLD A MODEL OF ITS OWN. Add one — an asserting model, in the F21 shape, doing nothing but `parentTotalAge = AgeGroupCensus.totalAge` — and every assertion in the document errors with `document holds several models; pass model_name`. The ingesting build takes exactly one model and `--model` narrows the report without reaching the build. Finding F45. So the mount is the whole of `models`, and per-fixture assertions cannot be inline.\n\n3. A MOUNTED CHILD'S OWN `metaparameters` DO NOT MERGE UP, but its `data_sources` DO. Measured both ways. The `data_sources` half is what lets a shared domain document carry the whole default-database catalogue while each fixture adds only its own runspec sources. The `metaparameters` half is what survived #298: the two names below are declared HERE because this document's `extent`s bind them and the loader API closes discovered extents at the ROOT, and they are declared AGAIN in the child because the child owns the axes they size. `bindings` cannot bridge it — it closes a name the child declares and cannot supply one it lacks. Finding F50, and it is the last thing between this shape and a per-fixture document of about twenty lines.\n\nTHE PATH IS ABSOLUTE AND MACHINE-LOCAL, deliberately. The default database is 1.1 GB and is not in this repository, so a `url_template` naming it cannot be repository-relative the way a snapshot path can. run-tests.sh SKIPS the spike stage when the directory is absent rather than failing it, which is how the fixture stage already treats a missing snapshot corpus.\n\nHOW THIS IS CHECKED. `run-tests.sh` emits this document's relation with `simulate --format csv` and diffs it against `expected/age_group_census.csv`, which is the fixture stage's own emit-and-compare pattern and the only verification F45 leaves available. It also asserts that `esm test` on this file finds ZERO assertions, so the day a mounted child's inline tests do run (finding F47) the suite says so instead of the diff silently remaining the weaker check. And it deletes the `metaparameters` block into a probe and runs BOTH tools against it — validate passes, the build fails — because F50 is silent at the first and loud at the second.",
    "authors": ["EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"],
    "tags": ["moves", "spike", "phase7", "run"]
  },

  "data_sources": {
    "spike_agecategory": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": { "url_template": "file:///scratch/ctessum/movesdb/movesdb20241112/AgeCategory.parquet" },
      "extent": { "metaparameter": "n_spike_agecategory" },
      "metadata": { "tags": ["default-db"], "note": "41 rows. The default database's agecategory table, unmodified and read in place.", "esio_format": "parquet" }
    },
    "spike_agegroup": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": { "url_template": "file:///scratch/ctessum/movesdb/movesdb20241112/AgeGroup.parquet" },
      "extent": { "metaparameter": "n_spike_agegroup" },
      "metadata": { "tags": ["default-db"], "note": "7 rows. The default database's agegroup table, unmodified and read in place.", "esio_format": "parquet" }
    }
  },

  "metaparameters": {
    "n_spike_agecategory": {
      "type": "integer", "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in agecategory. Runtime-discovered by `extent` (docs/esm-conventions.md §11), and the name this document's `extent` binds. The CHILD declares it too, and owns the axis it sizes; this copy is here because the loader API closes discovered extents at the root document and refuses a root that declares none (finding F50)."
    },
    "n_spike_agegroup": {
      "type": "integer", "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in agegroup. Same name in both documents, same reason."
    }
  },

  "models": {
    "AgeGroupCensus": { "ref": "./age_group_census.esm" }
  }
}
